    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d988">

                <pb facs="0961" n="703" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg703"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 841.731/295</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-01-28">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">January 28, 1915</hi>.</date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 2 p. m.</hi>] </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1541. Your 739, December 11.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d988-f1"/> British Government
                    now informs me that Meyer’s Atlantic cotton code, 39th edition, may be employed
                    in telegrams between the United Kingdom and any country on the extra-European
                    telegraph system. This does not include British colonies.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Ambassador</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
